> build · deepseek-v4.1-flash
Fixed. Here's what was wrong and what I did.

## Root cause

Your `pip` launchers were intact, but the actual `pip` Python package had been deleted from disk:

```
/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip/          <-- missing
/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info/  <-- orphaned leftover
```

So every `pip` command died with:

```
ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'
```

The leftover `.dist-info` also made `python3 -m ensurepip` wrongly report *"Requirement already satisfied"* and skip the repair.

## What I did

1. Moved the stale `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` metadata to `/tmp/opencode/pip-repair-backup/`.
2. Reinstalled pip from Python's own bundled wheel: `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip`.

## Verified working

- `pip`, `pip3`, and `python3 -m pip` all report `pip 24.3.1` (Python 3.13.1).
- `pip install --dry-run six` successfully resolved and downloaded from PyPI.
- A real install to a temp target downloaded, installed, and imported cleanly.

There was no `pip.conf`, proxy, or environment variable interfering — it was purely the missing package directory.

## One thing to be aware of

You have a *second* Python at `/usr/bin/python3` (Debian 3.11.2). It has no pip and no `ensurepip` module, and `python3-pip` isn't installed via dpkg. That's normal for a minimal Debian image rather than "broken." If you want pip there too, it should be installed through apt rather than ensurepip:

```bash
apt-get update && apt-get install -y python3-pip python3.11-venv
```

Want me to set that one up as well? Also note the deletion happened recently (site-packages was modified Oct 31), so if this is a container image build, whatever cleaned that directory may do it again.
